course: dictionary
lesson: 5841
title: A fast shark: mako
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mako is a large, very fast shark that lives in warm and mild seas. You do not need to know every word to understand a text. Look at the words around a new word, which are its context, because they leave clues. «The shortfin mako can leap several metres out of the water» tells you a mako is powerful and fast.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence: «Some sharks, like great whites and makos, must keep swimming to push water over their gills.» The words like great whites tell you a mako is a kind of shark, grouped with other large sharks.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Put shark in place of mako: «Some sharks, like great whites and sharks...» Does it still make sense? Yes, so a mako is a kind of shark. When the clues are not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Mako» is a noun: one mako, many makos, or a mako shark.
fact: «Mako» means a large, very fast shark that lives in warm and mild seas.
fact: The shortfin mako is the fastest shark, reaching about 50 kilometres an hour or more in bursts.
fact: «Bursts» means short, sudden periods of fast movement, so the mako does not always swim that fast.
exercise: «The shortfin mako is the fastest shark.» What kind of thing is a mako? | *A kind of shark | A type of seaweed | A kind of fish that cannot swim | A warm sea
exercise: «The shortfin mako can leap several metres out of the water.» What does leap mean? | Sink | Sleep | Swim slowly | *Jump up high
exercise: A mako lives in warm and mild seas. Where does a mako live? | In the desert | *In warm and mild seas | In cold, icy seas | In rivers, far from the sea
test: «Makos must keep swimming to push water over their gills.» Why must a mako keep moving? | To chase a boat | *To push water over its gills | To catch a bird | To stay warm in cold water
test: Which word names a large, very fast shark that lives in warm seas? | Nurse | *Mako | Jellyfish | Dolphin
exam: «The shortfin mako reaches about 50 kilometres an hour in bursts.» What does bursts suggest? | *Short, sudden periods of fast speed | Loud noises | A kind of food | Slow, steady swimming
